  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_19302d51362a45d98d6145a78488cf0a",
      "statement": "The error-genealogy method routes every raw trace error through five deterministic, LLM-free links — E0 granularity (what counts as one error event), E1 symptom (family + signature, description/provenance only, does not decide), E2 mechanism (the agent's actual mistake, the diagnosis), E3 unit (mechanisms grouped by a shared preventing lesson), and E4/E5 triage (candidate / non-memory / out, a decision rather than a taxonomy level).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://discussion/hipoteses/trace-error-taxonomy-methodology/general-error-taxonomy-methodology.md#L77-L85",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:cd209935d902e871e3d4d35cfb1af149962e7737b61d9720f80e9908cf0043a8:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_c658c83407f24ffaab49a6f25e906825",
      "statement": "Symptom (E1) and mechanism (E2) classification are two independent readings of the same error message with independent roles (describe vs. decide) rather than independently-coded pipelines; the mechanism axis adds exactly one local evidence signal the symptom axis never reads (e.g., the rejected code line), and the two axes relate many-to-many in both directions, so a tree cannot represent them — the symptom axis never feeds the triage decision.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://discussion/hipoteses/trace-error-taxonomy-methodology/general-error-taxonomy-methodology.md#L87-L108",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:52a2c64c466d452f487be9f0884040bd6249ac1a5c2c921f46717962e4424e8c:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_e67914fda8644ffba83bc6649c89b4d1",
      "statement": "The genealogy's edge cardinalities are symptom→mechanism = 1:N (the taxonomy divides: one surface complaint admits multiple causes), mechanism→unit = N:1 (the taxonomy fuses: different causes needing the same fix converge), and unit→decision = N:1 decided at the global unit level, not per (context × unit).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://discussion/hipoteses/trace-error-taxonomy-methodology/general-error-taxonomy-methodology.md#L110-L138",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:6a4cfa55bc548a070ec6f65c89db5be9062672ba8a3e29e5c77b842f8357ea9a:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_9a6cf9287ee440e49418ce7f75cb604d",
      "statement": "In the trace-law-flow instantiation, the 136 errors sharing the symptom signature 'Could not index' split into three distinct mechanisms via submecanismo() reading the rejected code line — dict_indexado_por_posicao (89), tipo_real_do_retorno (37), and campo_inexistente_no_retorno (10) — and the first two (plus a third mechanism born of a different symptom) converge into the same memory unit (U_contrato_dict, 96 errors) because one lesson prevents both, while campo_inexistente_no_retorno remains a separate unit because no single sentence could cover both causes without becoming a disjunction.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://analysis/2026-09-trace-law-flow/docs/09-metodologia-erro-a-memoria.md#L126-L140",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:42254a5e755487eb374e35a971d496f528c3736dad65d9b83fd5afeb754d2854:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_243c7f35b36b49d19ac18a74cb137894",
      "statement": "Writing memory at the symptom level instead of the unit level fails in two opposite ways simultaneously: a symptom that splits into multiple mechanisms produces an ambiguous, diluted card (a checklist of possible causes with different fixes, and a non-injective routing key since re-running the mechanism classifier would still be required), while a symptom whose mechanism is shared with another symptom produces a duplicated card (the same lesson written twice, doubling maintenance cost and risking divergence).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://discussion/hipoteses/trace-error-taxonomy-methodology/general-error-taxonomy-methodology.md#L253-L267",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2b39eccb6fef03102110632f6da2519f6b7b62ea7d1578c2b129ce92a86a7193:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_4aaebd4e8883409d9fcd1af40eaeb9c1",
      "statement": "A memory unit is only triaged as a candidate after passing three ordered gating questions — (1) is this the agent's own memory to learn from, as opposed to a harness/upstream-LLM failure routed to operational policy instead; (2) does the unit have unique, single-sentence content — residual buckets that fail this go to 'revisar', the taxonomy work-queue, rather than being discarded; (3) does it recur across executions and over time — and the recurrence thresholds (execution count, time window) are calibrated per instantiation rather than being fixed constants of the method, requiring an explicit sensitivity test.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://discussion/hipoteses/trace-error-taxonomy-methodology/general-error-taxonomy-methodology.md#L238-L262",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:10f53b579692309c6b0057b0f5832dda0821252eadaeb00849140c123ce84e7f:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_d5c17dbb03ba4e3d8decf8dcc1ddef71",
      "statement": "The method's construction order deliberately starts from the raw trace and only afterward tests the resulting taxonomy against the literature, which is restricted to three roles — testing the mapping against external taxonomies, suggesting analyses not yet run, and lending vocabulary — and is never allowed to supply the taxonomy's categories directly, since that would make the result an import rather than a genealogy grounded in the trace.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://discussion/hipoteses/trace-error-taxonomy-methodology/general-error-taxonomy-methodology.md#L56-L71",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:0984baf731d071d27b641ced72c776da313f671ebce1290d574aab0a9a564575:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_91a63aaa38054d359eb27ac6487bd2d7",
      "statement": "A prior version of the trace-law-flow memory-candidate table overclaimed that AgentDebug's finding was 'the module that produced the error routes the memory type'; checking the full 32-page PDF (arXiv:2509.25370) found zero occurrences of routing or memory-type language — the paper proposes a 5-module diagnostic taxonomy and an ablation showing root-cause-only correction beats fixing every surface mistake, but the routing claim was the project's own analogy, not a transferred finding, and was corrected in three files the same day.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://discussion/teoria/agentdebug-vs-trail-error-taxonomy.md#L7-L11",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:913974503cf09a22f72fc3235bb50a2e20ef96802c9eea762b7f93d4adca128f:"
        }
