    },
    {
      "id": "claim_ee4b18e432f145888f3656f40acc1e95",
      "statement": "AgentDebug and TRAIL classify agent errors along genuinely different, complementary axes rather than being redundant or competing: AgentDebug classifies by which cognitive module produced the error and outputs prescriptive correction_guidance, while TRAIL classifies by phenomenon (20 leaf error types) with a per-error impact severity field and a diagnostic output — AgentDebug informs the shape of a memory unit, TRAIL informs coverage measurement and prioritization by cost.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://discussion/teoria/agentdebug-vs-trail-error-taxonomy.md#L17-L35",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:7594f228d5dcdc9162d725ce2def4e51c6cc7a4141ccca2f66d935503c077b7e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_fecf5c7828974f4c95e2f46bd8a833c1",
      "statement": "The method declares six explicit limits: only exceptions that raise are counted (silent failures need separate detectors); the symptom axis's family/signature names already carry causal interpretation rather than being neutral surface labels; the method reads what the trace shows, not agent intent; there is no severity-of-consequence axis alongside frequency and cost; cascade deduplication looks only at a short window of consecutive steps; and triage recurrence thresholds are calibrated choices requiring sensitivity testing, not fixed constants.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://discussion/hipoteses/trace-error-taxonomy-methodology/general-error-taxonomy-methodology.md#L297-L316",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:d7006ecdbbcde827a8670fa80f2c6482d34db00998c70c9eb16d3288d4de3d54:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_8c2e981e1f2c41bdb2dae8f75e76ee20",
      "statement": "The classifier's residual is split into two non-overlapping buckets — 'Sintoma não reconhecido' (neither E1 nor E2 recognizes the message: a coverage alarm that asks for a symptom rule first) and 'Causa não identificada' (symptom known, cause unknown: asks only for a cause rule, and includes malformed-code noise until proven recurrent). Each error lands in at most one bucket, bucket size is part of an instantiation's coverage report, and both buckets pass the same recurrence test as candidates but counted per masked error pattern — a recurring pattern yields 'revisar — prioridade', otherwise 'revisar — baixa prioridade'.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://discussion/hipoteses/trace-error-taxonomy-methodology/general-error-taxonomy-methodology.md#L201-L219",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:87048d3791718caafc0b0162b8a8ab4a17fd4c617db8f5377b226fbfbe8b6387:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_9ab0b6e3159245048b946919f1baa161",
      "statement": "The single declared exception to 'the symptom axis never feeds the decision': montar_unidades() consults the classify() family only to separate the two residual buckets (causa_sem_regra + 'Sintoma não reconhecido' family → sintoma_nao_reconhecido) — buckets that never become memory; all 10 base-1 candidates are identical with or without that separation.",
